{
  "image_name": "b7a9395eb222adc2fcae955cdc562b9a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines a workflow for linking marine microorganisms with dissolved organic matter (DOM) through network analysis. On the left, microbial data are treated as one set of network nodes, derived from approaches such as 16S rRNA amplicons, metagenomics, metatranscriptomics, and metaproteomics. These microbial networks highlight hubs and “key microorganisms.” In parallel, DOM is represented as a second set of nodes characterized by ultra-high-resolution mass spectrometry for compositional diversity and nuclear magnetic resonance for structural diversity. This produces clusters and identifies “key RDOC,” likely recalcitrant dissolved organic carbon components.\n\nThese two layers are integrated using an MCP step and then analyzed together to build a bipartite complex network. In the final network, microbial groups sit at the center of subnetworks and connect outward to many DOM compounds. Edge colors indicate the direction of association, with orange for positive links and blue for negative links. Most connected DOM nodes are marked as RDOC/CRAM-like compounds, while a smaller number are labeled as other DOM. Overall, the image conveys how multi-omics microbial information and chemical DOM characterization can be combined to identify key microbes, key carbon compounds, and their positive or negative relationships.",
